const e={slug:"transitus-fluvii",title:"The Passing of the River",otherNames:["Scriptura transitus fluuii, the caption of Agrippa’s table (1533), and the words of de Balmes’s Latin heading (1523, sig. b6v)","the passing through the River (Agrippa’s text, in the English of 1651)","The writing called the passing of the River (the heading of the table of 1651)","Transitus Fluvii, or Passing the River, the usual modern names","Ketav ʿever ha-nahar (כתב עבר הנהר), “script beyond the River”, de Balmes’s Hebrew heading (1523, sig. b7r)","the transfluvial alphabet (Saverio Campanini’s term, as Kirsten Macfarlane reports it)"],family:"A ring-letter alphabet keyed to Hebrew: twenty-two signs, one for each Hebrew letter, in Hebrew order. It is the third of the three such alphabets Agrippa printed together in De occulta philosophia III.30, after the Celestial Writing and the Malachim, and the one of them printed before his book, in de Balmes’s Hebrew grammar (Venice, 1523) and in Panteo’s Voarchadumia (Venice, 1530). Macfarlane counts it among the ring-ended signs scholars call Brillenbuchstaben.",keyedTo:"hebrew",direction:"rtl",status:"ready",summary:"The Passing of the River, Transitus Fluvii, is an alphabet of twenty-two signs keyed one for one to the Hebrew letters, of the ring-ended kind scholars call Brillenbuchstaben. Agrippa printed it in De occulta philosophia, Book III, chapter 30 (1533), the last of three such alphabets, as a writing found among the Hebrews, and named no source. It had appeared ten years earlier in Abraham de Balmes’s Hebrew grammar (Venice, 1523), headed in Hebrew ketav ʿever ha-nahar and in Latin scriptura transitus fluuii, a script de Balmes says he found in a very old book; modern scholars trace from there the misreading that made it the script Abraham used when he crossed the Euphrates.",intro:["In the thirtieth chapter of his third book, after the Celestial Writing and the Malachim, Agrippa adds one more alphabet in half a sentence: “there is also another, which they call the passing through the River”, as J.F.’s English of 1651 renders his Latin “& est alia quã uocant transitus fluuii”. The table follows under the caption “Scriptura transitus fluuii”: twenty-two signs, one for each Hebrew letter. Agrippa says nothing more of it. He names no source, explains nothing of the name, gives it no use, and turns at once to the Cabalists’ cipher of the nine chambers.","His silence covers an earlier history. Kirsten Macfarlane, Joseph Peterson and the Rylands Library’s blog all point to a book printed at Venice by Daniel Bomberg in 1523, the Hebrew grammar, with a Latin version, of the Jewish physician and translator Abraham de Balmes, and the book bears them out. There it closes the section on the form of the letters. De Balmes has set out the square, “Assyrian” letter, remarked that scribes changed its forms from people to people and that only the Bible kept it, and given one common script as a sample, the script of the Jews of Spain; then he adds the River script under a single line, in Hebrew ketav ʿever ha-nahar and in Latin “Haec est scriptura transitus fluuii prout i[n]ueni in lib[ro] uetustissimo”: this is the script of the passing of the river (in the Hebrew, of Beyond the River), as I found it in a very old book (this page’s translation; the abbreviations are expanded, and the n that the print’s iueni lacks is supplied). The twenty-two signs follow on both facing pages, each under its Hebrew letter, and he gives them no use and no story. Macfarlane describes it as the first alphabet of ring-letters printed in Europe. That he counted it among the scripts derived from the square letter, as the Rylands blog reads its place, he does not say.","Set side by side, as de Balmes printed them on facing pages, the two names do not say quite the same thing, though none of the scholars cited here makes the point: the Hebrew names a place, “beyond the River”; the Latin names an act, “the passing of the river”. Saverio Campanini, whom Macfarlane follows, argues that Christian readers, not knowing the Talmudic background of de Balmes’s terse label, misread it. The alchemist Giovanni Agostino Panteo, at Venice in 1530, printed the same signs as his “second characters”, “Secundi uero in transitu fluuii subscripti: & Abraæ concessi”, the second, set down below, of the passing of the river, and granted to Abraham (f. 14r; this page’s paraphrase of terse Latin, with the printer’s contraction of concessi written out and the stroke over the æ left out); and, as Macfarlane tells it, many after him took the alphabet for the script Abraham used when he crossed the Euphrates, a river Panteo’s line does not name. Others read other crossings into the name: Claude Duret (1613) adds that the Hebrews say the letters were found on a statue by the Jordan, where the Israelites crossed it, as told in Joshua; a modern suggestion makes it the exiles’ return from Babylon; and the entry in Blavatsky’s Theosophical Glossary (1892), as an online transcription gives it, makes the river the Chebar of Ezekiel. None of these readings is Agrippa’s.","Websites that name Agrippa as the alphabet’s creator make him what he never claimed to be: he was its best-known carrier. From his book it passed to the English translation of 1651, to the engraved plate of Barrett’s Magus (1801), and, as the online text of Mathers’s Key of Solomon (1889) is reported to show, onto a pentacle on which some of the orders of angels are written in it. This page gives its signs as they stand in Agrippa’s table of 1533, traced from the Library of Congress copy, and shows that page beside them."],firstAttestation:{work:"Mikneh Avram (Peculium Abrae), a Hebrew grammar with a Latin version",author:"Abraham ben Meir de Balmes (c. 1440–1523), Jewish physician, grammarian and translator from Apulia",date:"18 November 1523 — the title page dates the printing XIIII Kal. Decembris 1523; the alphabet is read on its page, sig. b6v–b7r",year:1523,place:"Venice, in the house of Daniel Bomberg",locator:"Sig. b6v, the Latin page, and sig. b7r, the Hebrew page facing it, each with the table: in the Brandeis University copy (Internet Archive miknehavrampecul00balm, images n23–n24) and the same opening of the Florence copy (Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale di Firenze, CFMAGL. 1.6.42, images n624–n625, where a later hand numbers the Latin page 593). Joseph Peterson’s “sig. B6v” is the Latin page; the book itself has no page numbers, and the “pp. 24–25” of another citation match leaves 24–25 of the Brandeis scan, which is probably where they come from.",notes:"The earliest printing of the alphabet known to this page, ten years before Agrippa’s. The Latin page heads it “Haec est scriptura transitus fluuii prout i[n]ueni in lib[ro] uetustissimo” (abbreviations expanded, and the n that the print’s iueni lacks supplied) and the Hebrew “זהו כתב עבר הנהר כפי מה שמצאתי בספר ישן נושן” (without the vowel points it is printed with), each saying that de Balmes found it in a very old book; it stands in a grammar, not a book of magic, at the close of the section on the form of the letters, after the square letter and a sample of the script of the Jews of Spain. Macfarlane (2025) describes it as the first alphabet of Brillenbuchstaben printed in Europe. That de Balmes counted it among the alphabets derived from the Ashuri, the square letter, is the Rylands blog’s reading of its place; he does not say so. Agrippa does not name de Balmes. The link between the two books goes back to Teseo Ambrogio (Pavia, 1539, fol. 202v), who says that de Balmes’s grammar, printed by Bomberg, knows only two Hebrew alphabets, the common one and that “transitus fluminis” (of the crossing of the river: the site’s paraphrase), and turns from it to Agrippa’s Celestial and Malachim as further writings besides those two. Duret (1613, p. 125) quotes the passage, and the modern scholars make the link again. The signs bear it out, being the same twenty-two. Ring-letter alphabets keyed to Hebrew are reported in medieval Jewish manuscripts, but the sources consulted identify no manuscript copy of this one older than 1523."},drawing:{follows:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres ([Cologne?], July 1533), Book III, chapter 30, “Alius characterizandi modus, à Cabalistis traditus”: the table captioned “Scriptura transitus fluuii”, p. CCLXXIIII [sig. z3v], below the Malachim, twenty-two signs in rows of eight, seven and seven read from the right, in the Library of Congress copy, Rare Book and Special Collections Division, BF1598.A3 O4 1533 (LCCN 20007812), digitised as loc.gov resource rbc.2009gen12345, image 278.",drawn:"Each sign is traced from that page as the centre line of its printed strokes, with every ring centred on the printed ring and every stroke stopped at its ring’s edge. The drawing keeps what the cut shows: which ends carry rings and which are bare, where strokes join, the slant and curve of each, the loops and the one closed figure, and the signs’ sizes and places on the line. The site’s convention is one even pen for every stroke, where the cutter’s line swells and thins, and rings drawn a little larger than the printed ones and with a lighter pen than the strokes, so that they stay open at every size the site sets a sign, down to about 28 pixels. The cutter’s serifs and wedge feet are not drawn. Three small eyes are drawn one or two hundredths of the drawing’s width larger than printed, so that they stay open at the site’s pen: the cup under Caph’s bar, the loop of Res and the curl at the end of Tau’s bottom bar.",variants:"Compared sign by sign with two other copies of 1533 and two of the English of 1651. The McGill copy (Osler Library, Bibliotheca Osleriana 1746; Internet Archive, leaf n289) and the Jung copy (C. G. Jung’s library, e-rara 10.3931/e-rara-4309), registered to the Library of Congress signs, print the same cut stroke for stroke, and both label Teth as Theth. The English of 1651 (Wellcome Collection EPB/B/10377, p. 441, and a second copy digitised at the Internet Archive whose holding library is not stated) re-cut the table: the rows of eight, seven and seven of 1533 become rows of seven, eight and seven, running from right to left, without numbers or final forms. Its signs keep the forms of 1533, cut heavier and with larger rings, with a few changes worth knowing: Pe gains a fourth ring at its lower corner, Lamed becomes a sharper zigzag ending in a long tail, Vau’s right end turns up into a hook, and the curl that ends Tau’s bottom bar opens into a hook. De Balmes’s table of 1523 (sig. b6v and again b7r, Brandeis copy, and the same in the Florence copy) gives the same twenty-two signs in the same order, in rows of twelve and ten read from the right, each under its Hebrew letter, with differences of detail: Vau’s right end rises into a tall horn; Zain has a third ring at its point; the eye under the left end of Caph’s bar is a full round loop; Ain has a second ring at its foot; Zade’s middle and right legs hang about three-quarters of the way down the left leg; Res is a straight stroke with its loop, without the sweeping tail; Schin’s crook stands free between the arms; and Tau’s bottom bar ends in a ring. Panteo’s table of 1530 (f. 14r–v, Florence copy), cut heavier and coarser, gives the same twenty-two and agrees with de Balmes against Agrippa in Zain, Caph, Ain, Res, Schin and Tau, but not in Vau’s tall horn or Zade’s long legs. Duret’s Thrésor (Cologny, 1613), p. 124, read on a microfiche, re-cut the table with labels of its own (Olaph to Thau) in rows of eight, eight and six. Most signs keep the forms of 1533 in substance, Cheth gaining a ring at its foot, but eight are plainly different: Aleph is a reversed N, ringed at the top left and lower right; Vau a reversed-C curl ending in a ring; Lamed a zigzag on its side with a long tail; Mem a tilted rhombus; Samech an S-like hook with one ring; Ain a curl without rings; Res a heavy bow with a separate dot inside; Shin a V with one ring and no inner stroke. Fry’s “Chaldean 10.” (Pantographia, London, 1799, p. 34) takes Duret’s rhombus for Mem and an N-like Aleph drawn as the mirror of Duret’s, but keeps close to 1533 elsewhere. None of these is used as a base, nor is Joseph Peterson’s Transitus Fluvii font, reported to follow de Balmes, nor Barrett’s engraved plate of 1801."},plates:[{file:"magic/transitus-fluvii-loc.webp",width:1600,height:843,alt:"A woodcut table printed in 1533 under the heading Scriptura transitus fluuii: twenty-two signs, some ending in small rings, others plain bars, hooks, loops and frames, in three rows of eight, seven and seven read from right to left, each under the printed name of a Hebrew letter, on browned paper.",caption:"The table the letters above are drawn from: “Scriptura transitus fluuii”, in Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres (1533), Book III, chapter 30, p. CCLXXIIII [sig. z3v], below the Malachim, in the Library of Congress copy, BF1598.A3 O4 1533. Library of Congress, Rare Book and Special Collections Division.",rights:"The Library of Congress states that it is “not aware of any U.S. copyright or any other restrictions in the documents in this collection”.",url:"https://www.loc.gov/resource/rbc.2009gen12345/?sp=278"}],sample:{orders:[1,2,3,4,5],caption:"Aleph, Beth, Gimel, Daleth and He, set from the right as the table of 1533 sets them."},scriptNote:"Twenty-two signs keyed one for one to the Hebrew letters, in Hebrew order, with no final forms and no numbers of their own; the numbers shown here are the Hebrew hall’s. The names are spelled as the table of 1651 labels them — Cheth, Teth, Iod, Caph, Zade, Kuph, Res, Schin — and the transliteration is the hall’s. In the Library of Congress copy of 1533 the signs stand in rows of eight, seven and seven; in the English of 1651 in rows of seven, eight and seven, running from right to left with Aleph at the right; in de Balmes’s table of 1523 in rows of twelve and ten, also from the right, each sign under its Hebrew letter. That is the layout of a printed table, not evidence of the direction in which the script was written.",letters:[{order:1,name:"Aleph",value:"א",translit:"ʾ",numeric:1,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 82.2 74.2 L 56.3 51.0 C 47.5 43.4 37.5 35.4 31 31.4 C 26.4 28.6 20.4 28.8 18.3 32.6 C 16.4 36.4 18.6 41.6 23.2 44.6 C 27.4 47.3 32.6 47.9 37.9 49.0 M 63.4 34.5 L 56.3 51.0",rings:[[42.4,51.1,5],[65.4,29.9,5]]},strokes:"A long stroke from a pointed foot at the lower right runs straight up to the left, swings round in a narrow loop at the upper left and comes back underneath to end in a ring just below itself; a short arm from a ring at the upper right comes down to meet the long stroke at about the middle of its straight run.",note:"Labelled Aleph. In the table of 1651 it stands at the right-hand end of the first row, where a line of Hebrew begins."},{order:2,name:"Beth",value:"ב",translit:"b",numeric:2,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 40.5 28.3 L 67.3 27.4 L 67.3 73.1 L 40.1 73.2",rings:[[35.5,28.5,5],[35.1,73.2,5]]},strokes:"A top bar and a bottom bar, each starting from a ring at the left, joined at the right by a vertical: a bracket open to the left.",note:"Labelled Beth in 1651, where the Hebrew hall writes Bet; second from the right in the first row."},{order:3,name:"Gimel",value:"ג",translit:"g",numeric:3,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 61.7 29.4 L 62 50 C 62.3 63.5 58.5 70.8 46.4 70.9",rings:[[41.4,70.6,5]]},strokes:"A vertical comes down from the top and turns in a tight curve to the left, ending in a ring at the lower left.",note:"Labelled Gimel, as the Hebrew hall names it, and third from the right in the first row of 1651."},{order:4,name:"Daleth",value:"ד",translit:"d",numeric:4,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 35.2 29 L 63 28.6 L 64.2 72.4 M 35.2 44.4 L 63.4 44.2"},strokes:"A top bar and a middle bar of about the same length run from the left to a vertical at the right, which goes on down to the foot: a reversed F.",note:"Labelled Daleth in 1651, where the Hebrew hall writes Dalet; fourth from the right in the first row."},{order:5,name:"He",value:"ה",translit:"h",numeric:5,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 41.7 37.2 L 41.7 70.5 L 57.9 71.2 M 41.7 43 L 54.5 42.9 M 41.7 56.2 L 53.9 56.2"},strokes:"An upright with three arms to the right, like an E; the upright rises a little above the top arm, and the bottom arm is the longest.",note:"Labelled He, the Hebrew hall’s own name for the letter; fifth from the right in the first row of 1651. The upright rises a little above the top arm in all three copies of 1533, and is drawn so."},{order:6,name:"Vau",value:"ו",translit:"w",numeric:6,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 34.6 47.8 C 40 51.5 50 53.4 58 53.2 C 64 53 69.5 49.5 74.6 44.6",rings:[[30.6,44.9,5]]},strokes:"From a ring at the left a shallow curve sags to the right and rises again at its right end.",note:"Labelled Vau in 1651, where the Hebrew hall writes Vav and transliterates the letter w; sixth from the right in the first row. In de Balmes’s table of 1523 the right end rises into a tall horn, as high as the top of the line."},{order:7,name:"Zain",value:"ז",translit:"z",numeric:7,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 56.5 29.6 L 33.6 50.9 L 58.9 66.0",rings:[[60.2,26.2,5],[63.2,68.6,5]]},strokes:"Two straight strokes meet in a point at the left, like <, each ending in a ring at its right end.",note:"Labelled Zain, the hall’s Zayin. It closes the first row of the table of 1651, a row of seven. De Balmes (1523) and Panteo (1530) give it a third ring, at the point where the strokes meet; every copy of 1533 compared has a bare point there."},{order:8,name:"Cheth",value:"ח",translit:"ḥ",numeric:8,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 39.4 29.7 L 69.6 30 L 69.6 70.3",rings:[[34.4,29.7,5]]},strokes:"A bar from a ring at the left runs to the right and turns down into a long vertical.",note:"Labelled Cheth, the hall’s Chet. It opens the second row of 1651, the only row of eight in that table."},{order:9,name:"Teth",value:"ט",translit:"ṭ",numeric:9,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 65.1 29.6 L 65.4 64.2 M 39.2 45.2 L 65.2 45.3",rings:[[65,24.6,5],[65.5,69.2,5],[34.2,45.2,5]]},strokes:"A vertical with a ring at each end, and a bar from a ring at the left that meets the vertical a little above its middle.",note:"Labelled Teth in this table of 1651, though the Celestial table two pages before spells it Theth; the hall writes Tet. The table of 1533 labels it Theth. A pale fleck inside the vertical in the Library of Congress copy is a printing flaw."},{order:10,name:"Iod",value:"י",translit:"y",numeric:10,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 29.9 32.4 L 29.9 24 L 69.6 24.4 L 68.6 71.4 M 43.6 24.2 L 43.6 32 M 56.4 24.3 L 56.4 32"},strokes:"A top bar with a short leg at its left end, two short teeth hanging from it, and a long leg at its right end.",note:"Labelled Iod in 1651, where the Hebrew hall writes Yod and transliterates y; third from the right in the second row."},{order:11,name:"Caph",value:"כ",translit:"k",numeric:20,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 67.4 67.8 L 68.6 25.1 L 31.3 24.6 C 31.5 34.3 42.7 34.3 42.9 24.7"},strokes:"A bar with a small cup hanging under its left end runs to the right and turns down into a long vertical.",note:"Labelled Caph, the hall’s Kaf. The table has no sign for the final form ך, which the transliterator writes with this one and says so. The small cup under the left end of the bar is a half-ring closed by the bar, not a ring; it is drawn a little deeper than printed, so that it stays open at the site’s pen. In de Balmes’s table of 1523 the eye under the bar is a full round loop, on both pages, and Panteo’s of 1530 also closes it as a full ring."},{order:12,name:"Lamed",value:"ל",translit:"l",numeric:30,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 41.1 24.5 L 65.8 24.5 L 48.2 38.2 M 53 34.6 C 57 36 61.5 35.6 63.4 38.2 C 65 41 63.5 47 60 52 C 57 56.2 52 60.5 47.2 63.2 M 54.2 60 C 58.5 61 60 65 59.2 68.6 C 58.4 71.5 54.5 72.3 49 72.2",rings:[[36.1,24.5,5]]},strokes:"From a ring at the left a top bar runs right and turns back down to the left in a short diagonal; out of that diagonal a bowl swells to the right and sweeps back down to the left; from the foot of the bowl a small hook curls down and round to the left: a zigzag like a 3.",note:"Labelled Lamed, as the Hebrew hall names it; fifth from the right in the second row of 1651. The English of 1651 re-cuts it as a sharper zigzag: the round bowl and the closing hook of 1533 become straight strokes meeting in points, the last sweeping out into a long tail."},{order:13,name:"Mem",value:"מ",translit:"m",numeric:40,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 51.9 24 L 76 24.5 L 78 72.2 L 23.4 72.2 Z"},strokes:"A closed four-sided figure: a short top, a right side that is nearly upright, a long bottom, and a long left side slanting up to the right.",note:"The table gives no sign for the final form ם; the transliterator writes final mem with this sign and says so. The only closed figure in the table."},{order:14,name:"Nun",value:"נ",translit:"n",numeric:50,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 35.2 25.3 L 71.4 24.3 L 43.1 66.7",rings:[[30.2,25.4,5],[40.3,70.9,5]]},strokes:"A bar from a ring at the upper left runs to the right and turns back in a long diagonal down to a ring at the lower left, like a 7.",note:"There is no sign for the final form ן, which the transliterator writes with this one. Seventh in the second row of 1651."},{order:15,name:"Samech",value:"ס",translit:"s",numeric:60,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 42.6 35.2 L 65.8 24.8 L 66.4 69.6 L 45.3 74.7",rings:[[38,37.2,5],[40.4,75.9,5]]},strokes:"From a ring at the left a stroke rises to the right to the head of a vertical; from the foot of the vertical a stroke falls back to a ring at the lower left.",note:"Labelled Samech, the hall’s Samekh, and closing the second row of 1651. The Malachim table on the page before names Samech twice; this table names it once. The lower ring prints a little thick in the Library of Congress copy and clean in the McGill copy; its centre is taken from both. The small stray mark to the left of the upper ring is not part of the sign."},{order:16,name:"Ain",value:"ע",translit:"ʿ",numeric:70,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 49.9 33.7 L 50.5 60.8",rings:[[49.8,28.7,5]]},strokes:"A short vertical hanging from a ring at the top.",note:"Labelled Ain, for the letter the Hebrew hall writes Ayin. It opens the third and last row of 1651. De Balmes (1523) puts a second ring at its foot, and Panteo (1530) a curved stroke between two rings; Agrippa’s has one."},{order:17,name:"Pe",value:"פ",translit:"p",numeric:80,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 32.2 32.6 L 80 73.8 L 28.4 72.7 M 71.6 33.8 L 55.4 52.6",rings:[[28.4,29.3,5],[74.1,29.3,5],[23.4,72.6,5]]},strokes:"A long diagonal from a ring at the upper left runs down to a sharp point at the lower right and comes back along a horizontal to a ring at the lower left; a short arm from a ring at the upper right comes down to meet the diagonal near its middle.",note:"Labelled Pe, as the hall names it. The table has no sign for the final form ף, which the transliterator writes with this one. The English of 1651 adds a fourth ring at the lower right corner, where every copy of 1533 compared has a bare point."},{order:18,name:"Zade",value:"צ",translit:"ṣ",numeric:90,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 29.2 73.3 L 31.7 28.6 L 70.4 28.8 L 69.6 46 M 51.8 28.7 L 51.5 47"},strokes:"A long left leg rises to a top bar; from the bar hang a short middle leg and a short right leg of the same length.",note:"Labelled Zade, the hall’s Tsadi; the final form ץ has no sign of its own and is written with this one. In de Balmes’s table of 1523 the middle and right legs are long, reaching about three-quarters of the way down the left leg, on both pages; in Panteo’s of 1530 they are shorter, nearer the proportions of 1533."},{order:19,name:"Kuph",value:"ק",translit:"q",numeric:100,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 48.1 31.6 L 27.1 65.4 M 54.6 30.6 L 74.5 63.8 M 29.5 69.5 L 71.4 68.6",rings:[[50.7,27.4,5],[24.5,69.6,5],[76.4,68.5,5]]},strokes:"A triangle standing on its base, with a ring at each corner; each side runs from ring to ring.",note:"Labelled Kuph in this table, where the Celestial and Malachim tables of 1651 both spell it Kuff; the hall writes Qof."},{order:20,name:"Res",value:"ר",translit:"r",numeric:200,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 69 24.6 L 68.7 37 C 68.4 42 66 46 65 50 C 63.5 56 58.5 61 53 64.2 C 46 67.8 37 68.6 28.8 67.6 M 66.4 43.2 C 63 39.6 57.2 39 55.2 43.2 C 53.6 46.8 57.4 49.2 61 48.9 C 62.6 48.8 64.1 48.3 65.3 47.7"},strokes:"A stroke comes down from the top, ties a small loop on its left side, and sweeps round to the left in a long tail.",note:"Labelled Res in 1651, where the Hebrew hall writes Resh and transliterates the letter r. The loop is a closed eye made by the stroke, not a ring; it is drawn a little taller than printed, so that it stays open at the site’s pen. De Balmes (1523) and Panteo (1530) cut it as a straight slanting stroke with the loop on its left, without the sweeping tail."},{order:21,name:"Schin",value:"ש",translit:"š",numeric:300,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 28.7 30.9 L 46.8 66.6 L 70.8 30.9 M 37.6 48.4 C 43 41.5 49.4 34 49.3 29 C 49.2 25.4 46.4 24.3 44.3 24.4 C 41.4 24.6 39.5 27.2 39.6 31.4",rings:[[26.4,26.4,5],[73.6,26.7,5]]},strokes:"A V with a ring at the top of each arm; from the left arm, just above its middle, a crook rises to the right, arches over and hooks down to the left, ending free.",note:"Labelled Schin, in 1533 as in 1651. Of its sister tables of 1651 the Celestial has Shin, and the Malachim, whose labels as keyed in EEBO-TCP have no Shin, prints Schin on the page. In de Balmes’s table of 1523 the crook stands free between the arms on both pages, and so it does in Panteo’s of 1530."},{order:22,name:"Tau",value:"ת",translit:"t",numeric:400,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 77.6 39.3 L 77.6 27 L 25.8 26.8 L 25.8 71 L 83 71.1 C 88.4 71.1 89.2 78.2 84.4 78.4 C 81.8 78.5 80.1 77.4 79.8 76.4 M 12.8 57.3 L 12.8 50 L 54.3 50.1 L 54.3 42"},strokes:"An E-like frame of a top bar whose right end turns down, a left upright, and a bottom bar ending in a small curl; a middle bar crosses the upright, with a tick down at its left end and a short leg up at its right end.",note:"Labelled Tau. It is the last sign of the table, at the left-hand end of the third row of 1651. The curl at the end of the bottom bar is a tight eye in the print; it is drawn a little larger, so that it stays open. In 1651 it is an open hook; in de Balmes (1523) and Panteo (1530) a full ring."}],transliteration:{from:"hebrew",convention:"The Passing of the River is keyed to the Hebrew letters one sign for one, so the tool takes Hebrew only, with any pointing removed, and writes each letter with its sign. The table has no final forms, so the five final letters are written with the sign of the ordinary letter, and the tool says so each time: that is the site’s convention, not Agrippa’s. No Latin spelling is turned into Hebrew.",folds:[]},transmission:[{slug:"jewish-ring-letters",title:"Ring-letters set against the Hebrew alphabet",kind:"manuscript",year:1e3,end:1500,circa:!0,circaEnd:!0,when:"medieval",description:"Ring-ended alphabets keyed one for one to the Hebrew letters are reported in medieval Jewish magical and kabbalistic manuscripts, and Gideon Bohak follows such signs back to the charaktêres of ancient magic. Scholars place the Passing of the River in this family, but the sources consulted identify no manuscript copy of it older than its printing of 1523.",dating:"A period, not a date: the sources name no dated manuscript that holds this alphabet (Bohak 2011; Macfarlane 2025)."},{slug:"de-balmes-1523",title:"De Balmes’s Hebrew grammar: the earliest printing found",kind:"text",year:1523,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Abraham de Balmes’s Mikneh Avram, a Hebrew grammar with a Latin version printed by Daniel Bomberg, prints the alphabet twice, on facing pages, at the close of its section on the form of the letters, after the square letter and the script of the Jews of Spain: on the Latin page under “Haec est scriptura transitus fluuii prout i[n]ueni in lib[ro] uetustissimo”, on the Hebrew under “זהו כתב עבר הנהר כפי מה שמצאתי בספר ישן נושן”, the script of the passing of the river (in the Hebrew, of Beyond the River), as I found it in a very old book (this page’s translation; the Latin’s abbreviations expanded and the n that its iueni lacks supplied, the Hebrew without the vowel points it is printed with). Twenty-two signs, each under its Hebrew letter. Macfarlane describes it as the first alphabet of Brillenbuchstaben printed in Europe.",dating:"Venice, 18 November 1523 (XIIII Kal. Decembris, by the title page). Sig. b6v–b7r, read in the Brandeis copy and seen in the Florence copy. De Balmes’s dates are given as c. 1440–1523; a report that the grammar appeared after his death is unconfirmed."},{slug:"panteo-1530",title:"Panteo’s Voarchadumia: the River script made Abraham’s",kind:"text",year:1530,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Giovanni Agostino Panteo’s alchemical treatise, printed at Venice, sets out four sets of “characters” in tables with Latin values, numbered fourth, third, second and first: the fourth and third, two forms of the Hebrew letters, after which he writes that “the characters above” were granted to Moses on Mount Sinai (ff. 12r–13v); the second, this alphabet, under the line “Secundi uero in transitu fluuii subscripti: & Abraæ concessi” (the contraction of concessi written out and the stroke over the æ left out), granted to Abraham (f. 14r–v); and the first, “Antiquiores autem hi: & concessi Enoch”, older and granted to Enoch, an alphabet of twenty-six signs keyed to the Latin letters (ff. 14v–15r). Macfarlane, following Campanini, makes Panteo the first of many to read it as the script Abraham used when he crossed the Euphrates; Panteo’s line names Abraham and the river, not the Euphrates.",dating:"Venice, 1530 (April, by the library record). In the Florence copy the table is on f. 14r–v (sig. C2r–v): the twenty-two River signs, sign for sign, with Latin values; a blog post’s second-hand report that it mixes signs from all three of Agrippa’s alphabets is not borne out by this printing. The page numbers Wikipedia gives for it, pp. 545–46, do not fit the quarto of 1530 and probably belong to a later reprint."},{slug:"agrippa-1533",title:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia III.30",kind:"text",year:1533,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"The last of the three alphabets Agrippa gives as writings of the Hebrews, in the chapter “Alius characterizandi modus, à Cabalistis traditus”. In the Library of Congress copy its table, captioned “Scriptura transitus fluuii”, shares p. CCLXXIIII with the Malachim’s: twenty-two signs in rows of eight, seven and seven. The cipher of the nine chambers follows. Agrippa names no source, and mentions neither de Balmes nor Panteo, though his table gives the same twenty-two signs.",dating:"The colophon reads “Anno M. D. XXXIII. Mense Iulio.” (Library of Congress copy), July 1533, as the Eton and Utrecht catalogue records also give it. [Cologne] and Johannes Soter are the catalogues’ attribution, with a query. Four or five issues dated 1533 are reported. Three copies were compared for this page: those of the Library of Congress and McGill, which share one setting of these pages, and Jung’s, which has another; the rest were not seen. Book I was printed alone in 1531, without the alphabets of Book III."},{slug:"ambrogio-1539",title:"Ambrogio names the River alphabet",kind:"text",year:1539,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"In the appendix of alphabets of his Introductio in Chaldaicam linguam (Pavia), Teseo Ambrogio reports that Panteo’s Voarchadumia records four Hebrew alphabets, the third granted to Abraham at the crossing of the river (Panteo, numbering from the oldest, calls it the second), and that de Balmes’s grammar knows only two, the common one and that of the crossing of the river. He then turns to Agrippa’s Celestial and Malachim as further writings besides those two. Whether he prints the River signs has not been established: the two ring-letter alphabets on fol. 203v have not been compared.",dating:"Fols. 202r–202v, read in the copy of the Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale di Roma (Internet Archive bub_gb_yUkKq_QnrnwC); given here in the site’s paraphrase. Duret quotes the passage in 1613 (p. 125)."},{slug:"duret-1613",title:"Duret: the alphabet alleged to be Abraham’s",kind:"text",year:1613,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Claude Duret’s Thrésor de l’histoire des langues de cest univers (Cologny) prints the alphabet on p. 124, after the Malachim, as another Hebrew alphabet which, he says, is alleged to have been given to the Patriarch Abraham at the crossing of the river, when he left Chaldea for the land of Canaan (the site’s paraphrase). Its twenty-two signs, in rows of eight, eight and six read from the right, are labelled in italic from Olaph to Thau, with spellings unlike Agrippa’s: Gomal, Doleth (or Doluth: the fiche leaves the second vowel unclear), Lomed, Samach, Hoin, Choph for Caph, Coph for Kuph, Tzode. Most signs keep the forms of 1533 in substance, but Aleph, Vau, Lamed, Mem, Samech, Ain, Res and Shin are plainly different. On p. 125 he adds that the Hebrews say these characters were found on a statue on the bank of the Jordan, engraved with the names of the children of Israel when they crossed it, as told in Joshua, and that de Balmes confirms it, as Ambrogio reports. The Latin he then quotes from Ambrogio says only that de Balmes knew the alphabet, and de Balmes’s own page tells no such story.",dating:"Read on a microfiche of the edition (Internet Archive fre_b1886963): p. 124 (leaf n160; sig. Q2v by count) and p. 125 (n161, signed Q 3)."},{slug:"jf-1651",title:"The English Agrippa",kind:"text",year:1651,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule), translated by “J.F.”, probably John French, gives the chapter in English, where the alphabet is “the passing through the River”, and re-cuts the table under the heading “The writing called the passing of the River.” on p. 441: twenty-two signs labelled with the Hebrew letter-names, in rows of seven, eight and seven, running from right to left, with no numbers and no final forms.",dating:"Wing A789. Joseph Peterson is reported to warn that the translation adds errors of its own, which is why its signs are recorded beside those of 1533 rather than in their place."},{slug:"fry-1799",title:"Fry’s “Chaldean 10”",kind:"text",year:1799,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Edmund Fry’s Pantographia (London) gives the alphabet as “Chaldean 10.” (p. 34), with a Latin letter under each sign. His note on p. 35 reads: “Sigismond Fante says, that this alphabet is of very great antiquity, having been used by the Hebrews in the wilderness, in the time of Moses”, citing De Sivry’s Recherches (p. 191), and “Duret, p. 124, says, this character was given to Abraham, when he departed from Chaldea for the Land of Canaan.” His Mem is Duret’s rhombus and his Aleph an N-like sign like Duret’s, but drawn as its mirror image; his Vau, Lamed, Samech, Ain, Res and Shin keep close to Agrippa’s of 1533, so he did not take every form from Duret.",dating:"London: Cooper and Wilson, 1799; read in the Wellcome Library copy (Internet Archive b28757002)."},{slug:"barrett-1801",title:"Barrett’s Magus",kind:"text",year:1801,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Francis Barrett’s The Magus (London: Lackington, Allen & Co.), largely compiled from the English Agrippa, follows III.30 in Book II, chapter XVI, and an engraved plate sets four alphabets one above another: the Theban, the Celestial Writing, the Malachim and this one.",dating:"Westcott’s Flying Roll XXXII places the plate facing p. 64 of Book II. The plate’s number, its caption and Barrett’s exact wording have not been examined for this account."},{slug:"mathers-1889",title:"Mathers’s Key of Solomon",kind:"text",year:1889,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"S. L. MacGregor Mathers’s English edition of the Clavicula Salomonis puts Agrippa’s alphabets to work. On the pentacle of its Plate III, as the online transcription is reported to show, six of the nine orders of angels are named in ordinary Hebrew and the other three in the Passing of the River.",dating:"Title page 1889; the preface is dated October 1888. Known here from reports of the sacred-texts transcription, not from the printed plate."},{slug:"theosophical-glossary-1892",title:"Westcott’s copies, and Blavatsky’s river",kind:"text",year:1892,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"H. P. Blavatsky’s Theosophical Glossary, published after her death, has an entry “Fluvii Transitus”. As an online transcription gives it, the entry glosses the name as the crossing of the river Chebar, the river of Ezekiel 1:1, credits Agrippa with presenting the alphabet, and reports that W. Wynn Westcott had supplied copies of it, with the Malachim and the Celestial, to the transactions of the Quatuor Coronati Lodge, Ars Quatuor Coronatorum, vol. III (1890).",dating:"Known from the Teopedia transcription; neither the printed Glossary nor the lodge’s transactions has been examined for this account."},{slug:"campanini-2014",title:"Campanini, “The Quest for the Holiest Alphabet in the Renaissance”",kind:"text",year:2014,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Saverio Campanini traces the alphabet from de Balmes’s grammar through its Christian misreading and its spread from Venice into north-western Europe. He had earlier written a study of de Balmes’s grammar itself (Annali di Ca’ Foscari, 1997).",dating:"In A Universal Art: Hebrew Grammar across Disciplines and Faiths (Leiden: Brill, 2014). Known for this page from the volume’s description and from Macfarlane’s summary."},{slug:"peterson-font-2016",title:"Peterson’s Transitus Fluvii font",kind:"text",year:2016,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Joseph H. Peterson’s Esoteric Archives adds a Transitus Fluvii font, beside one of the Celestial, and is reported to say that it is taken from de Balmes’s grammar of 1523, not from Agrippa, and to issue it under a Creative Commons licence (CC-BY 4.0).",dating:"The fonts are reported as added on 7 February 2016; the page has not been examined for this account."},{slug:"macfarlane-2025",title:"Macfarlane, “Written in the Stars?”",kind:"text",year:2025,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Kirsten Macfarlane reconstructs how Christian scholars took ring-letter alphabets from Jewish manuscripts and came to treat them as alphabets drawn from the stars and written by angels. Following Campanini, she sets the Passing of the River at the start of that story in print.",dating:"Journal of the History of Ideas 86.3 (2025), 473–506. Known for this page from its abstract and short extracts."}],claims:[{claim:"Agrippa printed the alphabet in De occulta philosophia, Book III, chapter 30 (1533), the last of three Hebrew-keyed alphabets.",standing:"documented",evidence:"In the Library of Congress copy of 1533 the table captioned “Scriptura transitus fluuii” stands on p. CCLXXIIII, after the Malachim’s, with twenty-two signs in rows of eight, seven and seven; the OCR of the McGill copy shows the same layout. In the English of 1651 the chapter is “CHAP. XXX. Another manner of making Characters, delivered by Cabalists.”, and the table stands on p. 441.",sources:["Agrippa 1533, III.30, p. CCLXXIIII (Library of Congress copy)","McGill copy (OCR)","J.F. 1651, Book III ch. XXX, pp. 439–441 (EEBO-TCP A26565)"]},{claim:"Agrippa presents it as a writing found among the Hebrews, and names no source, inventor or book for it.",standing:"documented",evidence:"The chapter opens “Apud Hebreos plures characterum inuenio modos” (1533), in J.F.’s English “Amongst the Hebrews I finde more fashions of Characters”, and the alphabet comes in as one “which they call” by its name: “& est alia quã uocant transitus fluuii” (1533, p. CCLXXIII), “another, which they call the passing through the River” (1651, p. 439). Web pages that say Agrippa credits de Balmes are mistaken: the link to de Balmes is not in Agrippa’s text. It is made by Ambrogio (1539, fol. 202v) and, among modern editors, in Joseph Peterson’s note.",sources:["Agrippa 1533, III.30, p. CCLXXIII","J.F. 1651, Book III ch. XXX, p. 439 (EEBO-TCP A26565)","Ambrogio, Introductio (Pavia, 1539), fol. 202v","Peterson, Esoteric Archives, Agrippa Book III"]},{claim:"Agrippa invented the alphabet.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Wikipedia’s infobox names him as its creator, and hobby sites follow it. Against that, Agrippa gives it as a writing that others “call” by its name, and the alphabet is in print in 1523 (de Balmes, sig. b6v–b7r) and 1530 (Panteo, f. 14r–v), before his book. On the record available he is its best-known carrier, not its documented author.",sources:["Wikipedia, “Transitus Fluvii”","J.F. 1651, III.30, p. 439","de Balmes, Peculium Abrae (Venice, 1523), sig. b6v–b7r (Brandeis copy; Florence copy)","Panteo, Voarchadumia (Venice, 1530), f. 14r–v (Florence copy)","Macfarlane 2025"]},{claim:"The alphabet was printed in Abraham de Balmes’s Hebrew grammar (Venice, 1523), ten years before Agrippa.",standing:"documented",evidence:"The table stands on the facing pages sig. b6v (Latin) and b7r (Hebrew) of the Brandeis University copy, and on the same opening of the Florence copy: the twenty-two signs of Agrippa’s table of 1533, with small differences of detail, each under its Hebrew letter, headed “Haec est scriptura transitus fluuii prout i[n]ueni in lib[ro] uetustissimo” (abbreviations expanded, and the n that the print’s iueni lacks supplied) and “זהו כתב עבר הנהר כפי מה שמצאתי בספר ישן נושן” (without its vowel points). The title page dates the printing 18 November 1523. An early witness agrees in part: Ambrogio (Pavia, 1539, fol. 202v) says that de Balmes’s grammar, printed by Bomberg, knows only two Hebrew alphabets, the common one and that “transitus fluminis” (of the crossing of the river: the site’s paraphrase), though he does not describe its signs. Joseph Peterson’s note to Agrippa III.30 gives the page as sig. B6v, and the “pp. 24–25” of another citation match leaves 24–25 of the Brandeis scan. Macfarlane (2025) and the Rylands blog report it too; that it is the first alphabet of Brillenbuchstaben printed in Europe is Macfarlane’s finding, not tested here.",sources:["de Balmes, Peculium Abrae (Venice, 1523), sig. b6v–b7r (Brandeis copy; Florence copy)","Macfarlane, JHI 86.3 (2025), 473–506","Peterson, Esoteric Archives, Agrippa Book III","Rylands blog, “Speaking to angels”, Part II (2 February 2022)","Ambrogio, Introductio (Pavia, 1539), fol. 202v"]},{claim:"De Balmes showed the alphabet, in a grammar rather than a book of magic, as one of the scripts derived from the Ashuri, the square Hebrew letter.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"Half of this is on the page: it is a grammar, and the alphabet closes the section on the form of the letters, after the square, “Assyrian” letter, de Balmes’s remark that the common scripts changed its forms, and a sample of the script of the Jews of Spain (sig. b6v–b7r). The Hebrew name the Rylands blog gives, Ketav ever ha-nahar, is the page’s, כתב עבר הנהר. But de Balmes does not say the River script derives from the square letter: he says only that he found it in a very old book. That reading of its place is the Rylands blog’s (2022).",sources:["de Balmes, Peculium Abrae (Venice, 1523), sig. b6v–b7r (Brandeis copy; Florence copy)","Rylands blog, “Speaking to angels”, Part II (2 February 2022)"]},{claim:"Christian readers misread de Balmes’s terse Latin label because they did not know its Talmudic background, and so made the alphabet Abraham’s.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"The label is on the page, the Latin “scriptura transitus fluuii” facing the Hebrew “ketav ʿever ha-nahar” (sig. b6v–b7r); the misreading is Saverio Campanini’s reconstruction (2014), known for this page from Macfarlane’s summary and from a study of the Glagolitic “characters” that reports it. Which Talmudic passage he means is not stated in those summaries. The Talmud’s discussion of ketav Ivri, the old Hebrew script, and ketav Ashurit, the square letter (b. Sanhedrin 21b–22a), is an obvious candidate, but that is this page’s inference, not his stated argument.",sources:["de Balmes, Peculium Abrae (Venice, 1523), sig. b6v–b7r (Brandeis copy; Florence copy)","Campanini, “The Quest for the Holiest Alphabet in the Renaissance” (2014)","Macfarlane 2025","“The Characters (Χαρακτῆρες) of the Glagolitic Alphabet” (Redalyc)"]},{claim:"The alphabet was given to Abraham, as Hebrew was to Moses and an “Enochian” alphabet to Enoch.",standing:"legend",evidence:"This is Panteo’s assignment in the Voarchadumia (Venice, 1530): over the River signs, which he counts as the second characters, he prints “Secundi uero in transitu fluuii subscripti: & Abraæ concessi” (f. 14r, the contraction of concessi written out and the stroke over the æ left out); after the two forms of the Hebrew letters set out before them he writes that “the characters above” were granted to Moses on Mount Sinai (f. 13v); and of an older alphabet after them he writes “concessi Enoch” (f. 14v). His signs are this alphabet’s twenty-two. Ambrogio reported the assignment in 1539 (fol. 202r): Panteo, he says, records four Hebrew alphabets: the common one; one given by God to Moses on Sinai; a third, “Abrahe in fluminis transitu concessum”, granted to Abraham at the crossing of the river; and a fourth, older than the rest, handed down by Enoch (the site’s paraphrase). Ambrogio counts from the common letter and Panteo from the oldest, so Ambrogio’s third is Panteo’s second. In the sources consulted no one before Panteo ties the script to Abraham; de Balmes does not.",sources:["Panteo, Voarchadumia contra alchimiam (Venice, 1530), ff. 13v–14v (Florence copy)","Chioli, “L’alfabeto enochiano”","Ambrogio, Introductio (Pavia, 1539), fol. 202r, Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale di Roma copy"]},{claim:"The name refers to Abraham’s crossing of the Euphrates.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"Macfarlane reports that many readers, beginning with Panteo, took the alphabet for the one Abraham used when he crossed the Euphrates (a paraphrase of her article, which is in copyright). The reading fits an old explanation of ʿIvri, “Hebrew”, as one from beyond the River. Agrippa does not give it, nor does de Balmes, and Panteo’s own line names Abraham and “the passing of the river” but not the Euphrates (f. 14r).",sources:["Macfarlane 2025","Panteo 1530, f. 14r (Florence copy)"]},{claim:"The name refers to the Jews’ crossing of the Euphrates on their return from the Babylonian exile.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"A modern suggestion, made on Wikipedia with a “may”, and with no early source. It is not in Agrippa’s text, nor on de Balmes’s or Panteo’s page.",sources:["Wikipedia, “Transitus Fluvii”","J.F. 1651, III.30, p. 439"]},{claim:"The name refers to the river Chebar, where Ezekiel saw his first vision.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"Blavatsky’s Theosophical Glossary (1892) says so in its entry “Fluvii Transitus”, as an online transcription gives it. It is a nineteenth-century theosophical gloss, not an explanation found in the early printings.",sources:["Blavatsky, The Theosophical Glossary (1892)","Teopedia, “Fluvii Transitus”"]},{claim:"Hebrew ʿIvri, “Hebrew”, means one from the other side (ʿever) of the River, so that the Hebrews are those who crossed.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"One traditional explanation among several, not a settled etymology. Genesis Rabbah 42:8 is cited for three readings of “Abram the Hebrew” (Genesis 14:13): that the whole world stood on one side and Abraham on the other; that he descended from Eber; and that he came from beyond the River. Rashi follows the last, and the Septuagint renders the word ho peratēs, “the one who crosses”. These are the readings as commonly cited; the editions have not been compared for this page.",sources:["Genesis Rabbah 42:8","Rashi on Genesis 14:13","Septuagint, Genesis 14:13"]},{claim:"Agrippa already had the alphabet in his draft of 1510.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Trithemius’s reply to the draft is dated “the 8. day of April, An. M. D. X.” in the English of 1651, so a draft existed by then; but no evidence has been found that the alphabets of III.30 were in it, and the book was reworked and enlarged before it was printed. The documented date of Agrippa’s printing is July 1533.",sources:["Trithemius to Agrippa, 8 April 1510 (J.F. 1651, front matter)","Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Agrippa”"]},{claim:"Panteo’s plate of 1530 mixes signs from all three of Agrippa’s alphabets.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Contradicted by the printing of 1530. A blog post (Nick Pelling, Cipher Mysteries, 2009), reading an edition of 1550, reports at second hand that Panteo formed his “second alphabet” from Agrippa’s three. In the Florence copy of 1530 Panteo’s second characters (f. 14r–v) are this alphabet’s twenty-two signs in Hebrew order, each the River sign for its own letter, as in de Balmes’s table of 1523 (though not in every detail of form, as the notes on the variants say); the other alphabets on ff. 12r–15r are two forms of the Hebrew letters and Panteo’s alphabet ascribed to Enoch. Some River shapes recur in Agrippa’s other tables, the ringed triangle of Kuph being also his Celestial Iod, and Panteo’s coarse Ain, a deep curve between two rings, comes near Agrippa’s Malachim Iod; each still stands for its own River letter. The edition of 1550 was not seen.",sources:["Panteo, Voarchadumia (Venice, 1530), ff. 12r–15r (Florence copy)","Cipher Mysteries, 23 May 2009"]},{claim:"Geoffroy Tory printed this alphabet as “Lettres Chaldaïques” in his Champ Fleury (Paris, 1529).",standing:"attributed",evidence:"Wikipedia identifies Tory’s “Lettres Chaldaiques” with this alphabet and places them at f. 76v. The BnF’s own image record of the page “Lettres chaldaïques et lettres goffes” gives f. 73r, and says Tory presents the Chaldaic letters as the signs the Hebrews used in the time of Moses. The two disagree on the leaf, and the identification has not been tested against the book.",sources:["Wikipedia, “Transitus Fluvii”","BnF Essentiels, “Lettres chaldaïques et lettres goffes”"]},{claim:"The script is written from right to left, like Hebrew.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"Wikipedia says so, without a source. The tables of 1523 and 1651 do set their signs from right to left, Aleph at the right of the first row, but that is the layout of a printed table; no text written in the alphabet is cited for the direction of its writing.",sources:["Wikipedia, “Transitus Fluvii”","de Balmes, Peculium Abrae (Venice, 1523), sig. b6v–b7r (Brandeis copy)","J.F. 1651, p. 441 (EEBO-TCP A26565)"]}],uses:[{use:"An exhibit in a Hebrew grammar",evidence:"De Balmes (Venice, 1523) prints it at the close of his section on the form of the letters, after the square letter and the script of the Jews of Spain, as a script he found in a very old book, with no use attached (sig. b6v–b7r)."},{use:"Abraham’s script, in alchemical and cabalistic learning",evidence:"Panteo (Venice, 1530) sets it as the second of four sets of characters and calls it granted to Abraham. Before it stand two forms of the Hebrew letters, after which he writes that “the characters above” were granted to Moses on Sinai; after it comes an older alphabet, granted to Enoch (ff. 13v–14v)."},{use:"One of the Cabalists’ ways of making characters, as Agrippa presents it",evidence:"III.30 follows III.29, “Of the Characters and Seals of spirits”, and after the three alphabets goes on to the twenty-seven Hebrew letters in three classes, the nine chambers and a character made from the name Michael (1651, pp. 441–443). Agrippa attaches no rite to this alphabet. At the close of the chapter he says that angels bear no characters of their own, and that the figures men consecrate to them cannot compel them: they are those “by which we rise up to them” (1651, p. 444)."},{use:"Its family as evidence in a learned history of the Hebrew alphabet",evidence:"Macfarlane shows Athanasius Kircher setting the ring-letter alphabets inside his history of the Hebrew letters, in the Oedipus Aegyptiacus (vol. II, 1653) and again in Turris Babel (1679). Whether this alphabet is among those he prints, and where, has not been established."},{use:"Names of angels on a pentacle",evidence:"In Mathers’s Key of Solomon (1889), on the pentacle of Plate III, three of the nine orders of angels are reported to be named in this alphabet and the other six in ordinary Hebrew. The report rests on the online transcription; the printed plate has not been examined for this account."},{use:"A font with a named source",evidence:"Joseph Peterson’s Esoteric Archives offers a Transitus Fluvii font that it is reported to take from de Balmes (1523) rather than from Agrippa. Any use of the alphabet to write Latin letters is a modern convention, not Agrippa’s."}],quotations:[{text:"There is also a writing which they call Malachim, or Melachim i. e. of Angels, or Regal; there is also another, which they call the passing through the River, and the Characters and figures of all these are such.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), p. 439, as keyed in EEBO-TCP A26565",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia, Book III, chapter 30",note:"All that Agrippa says of the alphabet; its table follows two pages on."},{text:"Est etiam quam uocant scripturam Malachim uel Melachim, hoc est angelorum siue regalem: & est alia quã uocant transitus fluuii, & horum omnium characteres & figurae tales sunt.",language:"Latin",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"There is also a writing which they call Malachim or Melachim, that is, of the angels, or royal; and there is another, which they call the passing of the river; and the characters and figures of all of these are as follows.",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres ([Cologne?], 1533), Book III, chapter 30, p. CCLXXIII",note:"As printed in the Library of Congress copy; “quã” is the printer’s contraction of quam, and the ligature æ is written ae."},{text:"The writing called the passing of the River.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London, 1651), p. 441, as keyed in EEBO-TCP A26565",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia, Book III, chapter 30, the heading of the table",note:"The heading says “of the River” where the text two pages before says “through the River”."},{text:"Amongst the Hebrews I finde more fashions of Characters, whereof one is most ancient, viz. an Ancient writing which Moses, and the Prophets used, the form of which is not rashly to be discovered to any; for those letters which they use at this day, were instituted by Esdras.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), p. 439, as keyed in EEBO-TCP A26565",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia, Book III, chapter 30, the opening sentence",note:"The printed drop capital (“AMongst”) is normalised."},{text:"Now you must know that Angelicall spirits, seeing they are of a pure intellect, and altogether incorporeall, are not marked with any marks or Characters, and pingible figures, or any other humane signs; but we not knowing their essence, or quality, do from their names, or works, or otherwise, according to our fancies devote and consecrate to them figures, and marks, by which we cannot any way compel them to us, but by which we rise up to them […]",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London, 1651), p. 444, as keyed in EEBO-TCP A26565",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia, Book III, chapter 30, near its close",note:"Said of the characters men give to angels generally; the passage closes the chapter in which this alphabet stands."}],links:[{to:"hebrew",relation:"Its twenty-two signs stand one for one for the Hebrew letters, labelled with their names and set in Hebrew order; the numbers and transliteration on this page are the Hebrew hall’s. De Balmes printed it after the square Hebrew letter and the script of the Jews of Spain, each sign under its Hebrew letter (1523, sig. b6v–b7r)."},{to:"celestial",relation:"The first alphabet of III.30. In 1533 its table stands on the page before this one’s; in 1651 on p. 439, two pages before."},{to:"malachim",relation:"The second alphabet of III.30, named in the same sentence as this one. In 1533 the two tables share p. CCLXXIIII; in 1651 the Malachim stands on p. 440, the page before."},{to:"aiq-bekar",relation:"The Cabalists’ cipher of the nine chambers follows this table directly in the same chapter, III.30, in 1533 and in 1651."},{to:"theban",relation:"Printed in the chapter before, III.29, as characters “which Peter Apponus notes, as delivered by Honorius of Thebes” (1651). That ascription belongs to the Theban alone."},{to:"charakteres",relation:"The ring-ended signs of ancient and medieval magic, to whose family scholars assign this alphabet; no letter of it has been traced to a particular ancient sign."},{to:"pgm",relation:"Gideon Bohak (2011) follows the ring-ended charaktêres of Jewish magic back to those of Graeco-Egyptian magic, on his account the remote ancestors of the family."},{to:"phoenician",relation:"The Talmud contrasts ketav Ivri, the old Hebrew script of the Phoenician type, with the square ketav Ashurit. That discussion may be the Talmudic background Campanini finds behind de Balmes’s label; the connection is this page’s inference, not his stated argument."},{to:"enochian",relation:"Panteo sets this alphabet, granted to Abraham, before an alphabet he calls older and gives to Enoch, “Antiquiores autem hi: & concessi Enoch” (1530, ff. 14v–15r). Panteo’s “Enochian” is not the alphabet John Dee recorded in 1583."},{to:"agrippa",relation:"The author of the book through which the alphabet reached most of its later readers; not its inventor."},{to:"the-books",relation:"The chain of books that carried it: de Balmes 1523, Panteo 1530, Agrippa 1533, Ambrogio’s report of 1539, Duret 1613, the English of 1651, Fry 1799, Barrett 1801 and Mathers 1889."}],cautions:["The signs shown are drawings traced from one printing, the Library of Congress copy of Agrippa’s table of 1533, with the site’s even pen and rings: they keep the printed strokes and their places, not the cutter’s line, and they are not the forms of de Balmes’s printing of 1523, which differ in a few details (see how the letters are drawn). Where a drawing departs from the print, the letter’s note or the account of how the letters are drawn says so, and the page itself is shown beside them.","Agrippa should not be called the alphabet’s inventor, nor said to cite de Balmes. He names no source. The link to de Balmes is not his: Ambrogio made it in 1539 (fol. 202v), writing that de Balmes’s grammar knows two Hebrew alphabets, the common one and that of the crossing of the river, and going on to Agrippa’s Celestial and Malachim “praeter dicta duo alphabeta” (besides the two alphabets named: the site’s paraphrase). Modern editors and scholars have made the link again.","De Balmes’s printing of 1523 has been read on its page, sig. b6v–b7r. What still rests on modern scholars alone is that it was the first ring-letter alphabet printed in Europe (Macfarlane), that de Balmes meant it as a script derived from the square letter (the Rylands blog), and that Christians misread its label (Campanini). The “very old book” in which de Balmes says he found it is not identified.","Every explanation of the name is a later reading — Abraham’s Euphrates (from Panteo on, as Macfarlane reports, though Panteo in 1530, Ambrogio in 1539 and Duret in 1613 give the alphabet to Abraham at the crossing of the river without naming the river), the Israelites’ crossing of the Jordan (Duret, 1613, p. 125, as what the Hebrews say), the return from Babylon (a modern suggestion), the Chebar (Blavatsky, 1892) — and each is given here with its author. That the Hebrew name names a place and the Latin an act, which may be where the misreading began, is this page’s observation, not Campanini’s.","The explanation of ʿIvri as “one from beyond the River” is one of several in the rabbinic tradition, not a settled fact of philology.","The English of 1651 calls the alphabet “the passing through the River” in the text (p. 439) and heads its table “The writing called the passing of the River.” (p. 441); a quotation should give the page it uses.","Panteo’s table of 1530 has been compared: it is this alphabet sign for sign, coarsely cut and closer in most details to de Balmes’s than to Agrippa’s, and the blog report that it mixes Agrippa’s three alphabets is not borne out by that printing. It is not drawn from.","Locators repeated on the web are not secure: Wikipedia’s f. 76v for Tory disagrees with the BnF’s f. 73r, and its pp. 545–46 for Panteo cannot be leaves of the quarto of 1530, in which the alphabet is on f. 14r–v.","Kircher’s and Westcott’s copies are known only at second hand and are given here as reports. Duret (1613, p. 124) prints it, labelled, and Fry (1799, p. 34) gives it as “Chaldean 10.”; both are later witnesses, re-cut, and differ from 1533 at several letters. Ambrogio (1539, fol. 202v) names a “transitus fluminis” alphabet (of the crossing of the river: the site’s paraphrase) in de Balmes’s grammar, but whether he prints it has not been established, and no printing of it has been confirmed in Vigenère.","The Rylands blog reports ring-letter alphabets in Jewish magical manuscripts spelling divine and angelic names on amulets, but no source ties that use to this alphabet in particular.","Still uncertain, because the sources that would settle them have not been examined for this page: the “very old book” de Balmes names as his source; whether copies of 1533 other than the three compared differ further; Tory’s alphabet; Barrett’s wording and the caption of his plate; Mathers’s Plate III in print; the Theosophical Glossary in print; and the source of Peterson’s font."],sources:[{citation:"Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres ([Cologne: J. Soter?], 1533; colophon recorded by the catalogues as July), Book III ch. 30, pp. CCLXXIII–CCLXXIIII; Library of Congress copy, digitised.",url:"https://archive.org/details/DeOccultaPhilosophiaLoc1533"},{citation:"The same, Library of Congress catalogue record (LCCN 20007812).",url:"https://www.loc.gov/item/20007812"},{citation:"The same, Library of Congress copy, p. CCLXXIIII, the page the signs are traced from and the plate shown here, on loc.gov.",url:"https://www.loc.gov/resource/rbc.2009gen12345/?sp=278"},{citation:"The same, McGill University, Osler Library copy, digitised.",url:"https://archive.org/details/McGillLibrary-osl_henrici-cornelii-agrippae_folioA279o1533-19978"},{citation:"The same, the copy from C. G. Jung’s library (Stiftung der Werke von C.G. Jung, Zürich), digitised at e-rara.",url:"https://doi.org/10.3931/e-rara-4309"},{citation:"The same, Eton College Library catalogue record (printer “[J. Soter?]”; Adams’s suggestion of Basel).",url:"https://catalogue.etoncollege.com/B25098"},{citation:"The same, Utrecht University Library record (the colophon; four or five issues).",url:"https://objects.library.uu.nl/reader/1874-452079/metadata"},{citation:"Agrippa, Three Books of Occult Philosophy, trans. J.F. (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651; Wing A789), Book III ch. XXX, pp. 439–444, EEBO-TCP A26565.",url:"https://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/eebo/A26565.0001.001/1:18.30?rgn=div2;view=fulltext"},{citation:"The same, keyed text (CC0), Text Creation Partnership repository.",url:"https://github.com/textcreationpartnership/A26565"},{citation:"The same, page images of the 1651 edition (EEBO, via Jisc Historical Texts); the table of p. 441 is on image tcp:52070:236.",url:"https://data.historicaltexts.jisc.ac.uk/view?pubId=eebo-11994557e&pageId=eebo-11994557e-52070-1"},{citation:"The same, Wellcome Collection catalogue record.",url:"https://wellcomecollection.org/works/ftahuupv"},{citation:"The same, Wellcome Collection copy (EPB/B/10377), digitised at the Internet Archive: p. 441, the table of 1651 compared with the drawing.",url:"https://archive.org/details/b30335231/page/n454/mode/1up"},{citation:"Joseph H. Peterson, digital edition of Agrippa, Of Occult Philosophy, Book III, with notes (the note pointing to de Balmes).",url:"https://www.esotericarchives.com/agrippa/op3.htm"},{citation:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres, ed. Vittoria Perrone Compagni (Leiden: Brill, 1992); not consulted for this page.",url:"https://books.google.com/books/about/De_Occulta_Philosophia.html?id=dCARYHTxmeIC"},{citation:"Abraham de Balmes, Mikneh Avram / Peculium Abrae (Venice: Daniel Bomberg, 1523), Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale di Firenze copy (CFMAGL. 1.6.42), digitised by ProQuest (Early European Books): the opening sig. b6v–b7r, images n624–n625.",url:"https://archive.org/details/ita-bnc-mag-00000617-001/page/n625/mode/1up"},{citation:"The same, Brandeis University Library copy, digitised by the Internet Archive (2011): sig. b6v (Latin) and b7r (Hebrew), the pages of the alphabet, images n23–n24; title page n4.",url:"https://archive.org/details/miknehavrampecul00balm/page/n23/mode/2up"},{citation:"Giovanni Agostino Panteo, Voarchadumia contra alchimiam (Venice, 1530), Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale di Firenze copy (CFMAGL. 1.6.584), digitised by ProQuest (Early European Books): the four alphabets on ff. 12r–15r, the River script on f. 14r–v (images n39–n40).",url:"https://archive.org/details/ita-bnc-mag-00001097-001/page/n39/mode/1up"},{citation:"Dario Chioli, “L’alfabeto enochiano. Storia di una fantasia” (quotes Agrippa III.30, de Balmes and Panteo in Latin).",url:"https://www.superzeko.net/doc_dariochioli_saggistica/DarioChioliLAlfabetoEnochianoStoriaDUnaFantasia.pdf"},{citation:"Nick Pelling, Cipher Mysteries, “The Voarchadumia & John Dee” (23 May 2009): a blog, reporting a claim at second hand.",url:"https://ciphermysteries.com/2009/05/23/the-voarchadumia-john-dee"},{citation:"Geoffroy Tory, Champ Fleury (Paris, 1529), Gallica facsimile.",url:"https://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/bpt6k50961p.image"},{citation:"BnF Essentiels, “Lettres chaldaïques et lettres goffes” (Tory, Champ Fleury, f. 73r).",url:"https://essentiels.bnf.fr/fr/image/78729938-af73-44cb-98d6-82cefb245ce6-lettres-chaldaiques-et-lettres-goffes-1"},{citation:"Kirsten Macfarlane, “Written in the Stars? Alphabets and Angels in Early Modern Europe”, Journal of the History of Ideas 86.3 (2025), 473–506; abstract and extracts read.",url:"https://muse.jhu.edu/article/966903"},{citation:"The same, abstract, PhilPapers.",url:"https://philpapers.org/rec/MACWIT-14"},{citation:"Saverio Campanini, “The Quest for the Holiest Alphabet in the Renaissance”, in N. Vidro, I. Zwiep and J. Olszowy-Schlanger (eds), A Universal Art: Hebrew Grammar across Disciplines and Faiths (Leiden: Brill, 2014); known from descriptions and Macfarlane’s summary.",url:"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/The-Quest-for-the-Holiest-Alphabet-in-the-Campanini/1791c17c57290f343a6d8815b9f72c1ed6d1125e"},{citation:"A Universal Art: Hebrew Grammar across Disciplines and Faiths (Leiden: Brill, 2014), Google Books record.",url:"https://books.google.com/books/about/A_Universal_Art_Hebrew_Grammar_across_Di.html?id=Xk_3AwAAQBAJ"},{citation:"Saverio Campanini, “Peculium Abrae. La grammatica ebraico-latina di Avraham de Balmes”, Annali di Ca’ Foscari 36 (1997), 5–49."},{citation:"University of Manchester Library, Rylands blog, “Speaking to angels: Charaktêres in Jewish magical manuscripts”, Part II (2 February 2022): de Balmes’s alphabet.",url:"https://rylandscollections.com/2022/02/02/speaking-to-angels-charakteres-in-jewish-magical-manuscripts-part-ii/"},{citation:"Gideon Bohak, “The Charaktêres in Ancient and Medieval Jewish Magic”, Acta Classica Universitatis Scientiarum Debreceniensis 47 (2011), 25–44.",url:"https://ojs.lib.unideb.hu/classica/article/view/8015"},{citation:"“The Characters (Χαρακτῆρες) of the Glagolitic Alphabet: New Light on an Old Puzzle”, Redalyc (reports Campanini on the label scriptura transitus fluuii)."},{citation:"Francis Barrett, The Magus, or Celestial Intelligencer (London: Lackington, Allen & Co., 1801), the Wellcome copy on Wikimedia Commons.",url:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Magus,_or_Celestial_Intelligencer_(1801,_Francis_Barrett,_b22006795).pdf"},{citation:"The same, Book II ch. XVI, “Another Way of Making Characters, According to the Cabalists” (sacred-texts transcription).",url:"https://www.sacred-texts.com/grim/magus/ma228.htm"},{citation:"The same, the plate of four alphabets (Source Library).",url:"https://sourcelibrary.org/artwork/magische-alphabete-barrett-the-magus"},{citation:"W. Wynn Westcott, Flying Roll XXXII, “Theban Letters” (on the place of Barrett’s plate), as reported."},{citation:"S. L. MacGregor Mathers (ed.), The Key of Solomon the King (London, 1889), Plate III (sacred-texts transcription).",url:"https://sacred-texts.com/book/the-key-of-solomon-the-king/plate-iii"},{citation:"The same, Joseph H. Peterson’s edition at Esoteric Archives.",url:"https://www.esotericarchives.com/solomon/ksol.htm"},{citation:"H. P. Blavatsky, The Theosophical Glossary (London, 1892), entry “Fluvii Transitus”, Teopedia transcription.",url:"https://en.teopedia.org/lib/Fluvii_Transitus"},{citation:"Joseph H. Peterson, Esoteric Archives, fonts (a Transitus Fluvii font reported to follow de Balmes, 1523).",url:"https://www.esotericarchives.com/fonts/"},{citation:"Esoteric Archives, “What’s New” (the fonts reported as added on 7 February 2016).",url:"https://www.esotericarchives.com/whatsnew.htm"},{citation:"Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa von Nettesheim” (the draft of 1510 and the printings).",url:"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/agrippa-nettesheim/"},{citation:"Wikipedia, “Transitus Fluvii” (a pointer only: de Balmes, sig. B6v; Panteo; Tory, f. 76v; the modern explanations of the name).",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transitus_Fluvii"},{citation:"Genesis Rabbah 42:8 and Rashi on Genesis 14:13, on “Abram the Hebrew”; the Septuagint at Genesis 14:13."},{citation:"Teseo Ambrogio, Introductio in Chaldaicam linguam (Pavia, 1539), fols. 202r–202v, Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale di Roma copy, digitised.",url:"https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_yUkKq_QnrnwC"},{citation:"Claude Duret, Thrésor de l’histoire des langues de cest univers (Cologny: Matthieu Berjon, 1613), pp. 124–125, read on a University of Ottawa microfiche digitised at the Internet Archive (leaves n160–n161).",url:"https://archive.org/details/fre_b1886963"},{citation:"Edmund Fry, Pantographia (London: Cooper and Wilson, 1799), p. 34, “Chaldean 10.”, and p. 35, Wellcome Library copy digitised at the Internet Archive (b28757002; Public Domain Mark).",url:"https://archive.org/details/b28757002"}]};export{e as default};
